  #line-at(38.835pt, 462.604pt, "velocity-only inversion (c)–(d) but with a different orientation. In both cases, the recovered solution is poorly localised")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 474.559pt, "and strays away from the ground-truth. This is consistent with the local sensitivity analysis, where a single observable")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 486.514pt, "provides only one dominant constraint and is therefore insufficient to resolve both parameters reliably. When attenuation")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 498.47pt, "and velocity are used together in (e)–(f), the objective surface becomes more compact, with a well-localised minimum")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 510.425pt, "at the ground-truth parameter location.")
  #line-at(53.779pt, 522.38pt, "Overall, the inversion results confirm that accurate recovery requires complementary constraint across parameter")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 534.335pt, "directions: single observables yield weakly localised solutions, whereas the combined use of attenuation and velocity")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 546.29pt, "leads to substantially better recovery. Hence, inversion quality depends not only on the magnitude of sensitivity, but")
  #line-at(38.835pt, 558.245pt, "also on whether the chosen observables constrain complementary directions in the parameter space.")
